  {
    "episode_id": 73238,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/3/73238.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 42,
    "turn_text": "But being the eldest, my grandfather, by the time I was in my six, seven, eight years old, he had retired. And we would go visit with them, and my grandfather had a routine every morning of taking a walk with his friends, his buddies, who were all also retired, all these old men, who would take their walk in the morning and discuss the glory days, and then talk about politics. And it was a great honor, and people in the family would talk about that he wouldn't let anybody go on that walk with him, but he'd let me go on the walk with him. And I'd hold his hand as he'd walk with his buddies, talking about the need for an honest government. the need to wipe out corruption, a representative government. And I realized later how much that really, how much I absorbed it and it influenced me. And so that was a lot of the influence. In addition to just it being a culture, as I have experienced it, that is a very welcoming culture. It is a culture that is without judgment and is really I think it's about really understanding the things that one must aspire to be. You know, one of the highest callings that you can have is to be a studied and learned person. Money is not a value that one aspires to. It's about knowledge. And I think that's a very noble aspiration.",
