    "turn_text": "And to make the decisions. And also to not, again, accept the false choice. Look, I will never make an excuse for saying this, or apology for saying this. One human being kills another human being, a woman is raped, a child is molested, there needs to be serious consequence and accountability. And I'm going to always say that. And I'm going to say America has a problem with mass incarceration. We have been locking up black and brown men in particular. We have built in biases that are implicit and explicit that need to be addressed. And I talk a lot about all of those issues in the book. But even as Attorney General, I was able to create the first implicit bias and procedural justice initiative, which is about training police officers around implicit bias, the first one in the nation. And I had the power to do that because I was the attorney general. And so I would encourage folks that when you think about what is available to you in terms of reaching your goal, don't accept anyone's limitations about how you can do that. You can be in the room at the table where the decisions are being made, or you can be outside and be equally effective, but don't exclude yourself from the opportunity to be in the room where the decisions are being made.",
