    "turn_text": "election night, election night, as I write about all of my election nights in the book, election night, we have a tradition of family and friends, small dinner, and after the polls close and then go to where all of the friends and the supporters are for the election night, hopefully party. And so I went to the place where we were, which is the Lancy Street, which is a place that I have gone to almost every election night. It's a wonderful residential treatment facility for former offenders and getting them training jobs. So I urge you to look it up if you're at all interested in successful recidivism prevention programs in any event. Got there. And my consultant said, it's going to be a long night. And you know, but you probably should just get out there because the TVs are out there, they're on the risers. It was about 10 o'clock. He said, you should probably get out there and say something. But it's going to be a long night. And I said, all right. So I was in a little ante room. And everybody was in the big room. And so I walk out into the big room. And all of the supporters, lots and lots of people are there. And I'm walking through the crowd to get to the stage. And people are all tiered up. And I was like, oh, this was an incredible election. Against odds, everybody was fighting. We're in it. Everybody's feeling really emotional. I was very touched. And I walked up to the stage and I didn't have any notes. And I just said, you know, it's going to be a long night. But this is what we stood for and about the smart Encon. And I could feel the mood and the room kind of shifting. And then I was done speaking, and somebody who had been with me for a long time, she came up to the stage, and I was standing on the stage. She came up and she said, get back in the room. And you know, when you've been through battle with folks and somebody says to you do something, you don't ask why. So I said, OK, so I took the steps down. And then there was this one reporter. I'll never forget her. I see her sometimes in San Francisco. And she had the cameraman there. And she had this mic in my face as I was walking into the back room. She's like, well, what happened? What do you think happened? I said, we ran a great race. This is what we are about. This is what we stand for. And she and I are not connecting. And I get into the back room. And then I realized that the San Francisco Chronicle had declared my defeat. So I was the only one in the room who",
